Good. Now I have a complete understanding of both files. Here is the comprehensive implementation plan.

---

## Implementation Plan: Fix Orchestration Page End-to-End

This plan addresses all 14 identified issues, organized by priority tier. Each fix specifies the exact location, the change, and the rationale.

---

### TIER 1: Flow-Critical Fixes (Issues 1, 2, 3, 13)

---

#### Fix 1 + 13: Skip Start/Task Nodes on Backend (Avoid Wasted Claude Calls)

**File:** `/Users/yashbaldha/Desktop/Boldteq App/claude-hub/src/server.js`
**Location:** Lines 852-887 (the `for (const nodeId of order)` loop inside the `/api/orchestrations/run` handler)

**Change:** After looking up the node at line 854, add a guard clause:

```js
const node = nodeMap[nodeId];
if (!node) continue;

// NEW: Skip start/task nodes — they carry no instructions and only provide the task as context
if (node.data?.isStart) {
  results[nodeId] = task; // Store task text so downstream nodes can reference it
  send({ type: 'done', nodeId, label: node.data?.label || nodeId, output: task, skipped: true });
  continue;
}
```

Insert this immediately after line 855 (`if (!node) continue;`), before the `agentName` assignment at line 857.

**Why:** Currently, `isStart` nodes get a prompt like `"## Task:\n${task}\n\nProvide your response:"` and that gets sent to `runClaudeSync()`. This is a wasted Claude API call that simply parrots the task back. By storing `task` directly in `results[nodeId]`, downstream nodes still receive the context via the `incomingEdges` logic at lines 864-873 without the unnecessary spawn.

---

#### Fix 2: Make Toolbar "Run" Button Directly Run (or Open Panel Pre-Filled)

**File:** `/Users/yashbaldha/Desktop/Boldteq App/claude-hub/client/src/pages/Orchestration.tsx`
**Location:** Lines 511-518 (the Run button `onClick`)

**Change:** Replace the toolbar Run button behavior. The current `onClick={() => setShowRunPanel(!showRunPanel)}` should become:

```tsx
onClick={() => {
  if (running) {
    // If already running, just show the panel
    setShowRunPanel(true);
    return;
  }
  if (!showRunPanel) {
    // Open the run panel so user can enter/review task
    setShowRunPanel(true);
  } else if (task.trim()) {
    // Panel is already open and task is filled — run immediately
    handleRun();
  }
  // If panel is open but no task, it stays open (user needs to type)
}}
```

Also change the button text at line 517 to be contextual:

```tsx
{running ? (
  <><Loader className="w-3.5 h-3.5 animate-spin" /> Running…</>
) : showRunPanel && task.trim() ? (
  <><Play className="w-3.5 h-3.5" /> Run Now</>
) : (
  <><Play className="w-3.5 h-3.5" /> Run</>
)}
```

**Why:** The current two-step (click Run to toggle panel, then click Start Run) is confusing. This makes the toolbar button progressive: first click opens panel, second click (with task) runs. The panel's "Start Run" button still works as an alternative.

---

#### Fix 3: Allow Node Inspection During and After Run

**File:** `/Users/yashbaldha/Desktop/Boldteq App/claude-hub/client/src/pages/Orchestration.tsx`
**Location:** Line 416

**Current code:**
```ts
const showNodeEdit = !!selectedNode && !showRunPanel
```

**Change to:**
```ts
const showNodeEdit = !!selectedNode
```

Then change the right panel layout (lines 580-732) from the current mutually-exclusive approach to a stacked/tabbed approach. Replace the structure so both panels can coexist:

1. When `showRunPanel` is true AND `selectedNode` is truthy, render both panels. The run panel stays as-is on the right. The node edit panel renders as a narrower overlay/drawer on top or inside the run panel as a collapsible section.

**Simpler approach (recommended):** Keep them mutually exclusive for positioning BUT add a "node output viewer" that works INSIDE the run panel. When user clicks a completed node during/after a run, show its output in a modal overlay instead of the edit panel.

Add a new state variable:
```ts
const [viewingOutputNodeId, setViewingOutputNodeId] = useState<string | null>(null)
```

Modify `onNodeClick` (line 199-201):
```ts
const onNodeClick = useCallback((_: React.MouseEvent, node: Node) => {
  const nd = node.data as AgentNodeData;
  if (showRunPanel && (nd.status === 'done' || nd.status === 'error')) {
    // During/after run, clicking a node shows its full output
    setViewingOutputNodeId(node.id);
  } else {
    setSelectedNodeId(node.id);
  }
}, [showRunPanel])
```

Then add a full-output modal (see Fix 4 below, which shares this modal).

**Why:** Users need to inspect node outputs after a run completes. The current `!showRunPanel` condition completely blocks node inspection while the run panel is open.

---

### TIER 2: Output Viewer and SSE Robustness (Issues 4, 5)

---

#### Fix 4: Add Full Output Viewer Modal

**File:** `/Users/yashbaldha/Desktop/Boldteq App/claude-hub/client/src/pages/Orchestration.tsx`

**Add state** (near line 169):
```ts
const [viewingOutputNodeId, setViewingOutputNodeId] = useState<string | null>(null)
```

**Add computed value** (near line 177):
```ts
const viewingNode = nodes.find(n => n.id === viewingOutputNodeId) ?? null
const viewingOutput = viewingNode ? (viewingNode.data as AgentNodeData).output : null
```

**Add to lucide-react imports** (line 21-23): Add `Maximize2` icon.

**Make output clickable in AgentNode** (lines 89-93): Wrap the output preview `<p>` in a clickable container. Since AgentNode cannot access parent state directly, use a different approach: make the output line in the run log clickable instead.

**In the run log** (lines 695-710), make each "done" entry clickable:
```tsx
{log.type === 'done' && log.output && (
  <button
    onClick={() => setViewingOutputNodeId(log.nodeId)}
    className="text-[10px] text-accent hover:underline mt-1"
  >
    View full output
  </button>
)}
```

**Also make the node output section clickable.** Since `AgentNode` is a ReactFlow custom node component and cannot call `setViewingOutputNodeId` directly, add the `executionOrder` and click-to-view via a wrapper approach. Add `onExpandOutput` to `AgentNodeData`:

Actually, the simpler approach: clicking a completed node during/after run (handled in Fix 3's `onNodeClick` change) opens the output viewer modal.

**Add the output viewer modal** (after the Templates Modal, around line 792):
```tsx
{viewingOutputNodeId && viewingNode && viewingOutput && (
  <div className="fixed inset-0 z-50 flex items-center justify-center">
    <div className="absolute inset-0 bg-black/40" onClick={() => setViewingOutputNodeId(null)} />
    <div className="relative bg-surface border border-border rounded-2xl w-[640px] max-h-[80vh] shadow-2xl flex flex-col">
      <div className="flex items-center justify-between px-5 py-3.5 border-b border-border shrink-0">
        <div className="flex items-center gap-2">
          <CheckCircle className="w-4 h-4 text-green" />
          <h3 className="text-sm font-semibold">{(viewingNode.data as AgentNodeData).label} — Output</h3>
        </div>
        <div className="flex items-center gap-2">
          <button
            onClick={() => copyToClipboard(viewingOutput)}
            className="flex items-center gap-1.5 text-xs text-text-muted hover:text-text px-2 py-1 rounded-lg hover:bg-surface-2"
          >
            <Copy className="w-3.5 h-3.5" /> Copy
          </button>
          <button onClick={() => setViewingOutputNodeId(null)} className="p-1 rounded text-text-muted hover:text-text">
            <X className="w-4 h-4" />
          </button>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div className="flex-1 overflow-y-auto px-5 py-4">
        <pre className="text-sm text-text whitespace-pre-wrap leading-relaxed">{viewingOutput}</pre>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
)}
```

**Why:** Currently output is truncated to 3 lines in nodes and 4 lines in the run log with no way to see the full text. This provides a full-screen-ish modal with copy support.

---

#### Fix 5: Robust SSE Buffer Flush

**File:** `/Users/yashbaldha/Desktop/Boldteq App/claude-hub/client/src/pages/Orchestration.tsx`
**Location:** Lines 388-395 (the flush after the read loop ends)

**Current code:**
```ts
const remaining = sseBufferRef.current.trim()
if (remaining.startsWith('data: ')) {
  try {
    const event = JSON.parse(remaining.slice(6))
    setRunLog(log => [...log, event])
  } catch {}
}
```

**Change to:** Process ALL remaining lines, not just one:
```ts
const remaining = sseBufferRef.current.trim()
if (remaining) {
  const lines = remaining.split('\n')
  for (const line of lines) {
    const trimmed = line.trim()
    if (!trimmed.startsWith('data: ')) continue
    try {
      const event = JSON.parse(trimmed.slice(6))
      setRunLog(log => [...log, event])
      if (event.type === 'start' && event.nodeId) {
        setNodes(nds => nds.map(n =>
          n.id === event.nodeId ? { ...n, data: { ...n.data, status: 'running' } } : n
        ))
      }
      if (event.type === 'done' && event.nodeId) {
        setNodes(nds => nds.map(n =>
          n.id === event.nodeId ? { ...n, data: { ...n.data, status: 'done', output: event.output } } : n
        ))
      }
      if (event.type === 'error' && event.nodeId) {
        setNodes(nds => nds.map(n =>
          n.id === event.nodeId ? { ...n, data: { ...n.data, status: 'error' } } : n
        ))
      }
    } catch {}
  }
}
```

**Why:** The current flush only handles a single event. If the final chunk contains multiple `data:` lines (e.g., the last node's "done" plus the "complete" event), only the first one gets processed. The "complete" event could be lost, meaning `finalOutput` never appears.

Consider extracting the event-processing logic into a helper function to avoid duplicating the `setNodes` calls:

```ts
// Add as a local function inside the component, near line 327:
const processSSEEvent = useCallback((event: RunLog) => {
  setRunLog(log => [...log, event])
  if (event.type === 'start' && event.nodeId) {
    setNodes(nds => nds.map(n =>
      n.id === event.nodeId ? { ...n, data: { ...n.data, status: 'running' } } : n
    ))
  }
  if (event.type === 'done' && event.nodeId) {
    setNodes(nds => nds.map(n =>
      n.id === event.nodeId ? { ...n, data: { ...n.data, status: 'done', output: event.output } } : n
    ))
  }
  if (event.type === 'error' && event.nodeId) {
    setNodes(nds => nds.map(n =>
      n.id === event.nodeId ? { ...n, data: { ...n.data, status: 'error' } } : n
    ))
  }
}, [setNodes])
```

Then use `processSSEEvent(event)` both inside the main read loop (lines 366-384) and in the flush section.

---

### TIER 3: UX Polish (Issues 6, 7, 8, 9, 10, 11, 12)

---

#### Fix 6: Delete Key on Mac

**File:** `/Users/yashbaldha/Desktop/Boldteq App/claude-hub/client/src/pages/Orchestration.tsx`
**Location:** Line 561

**Current:** `deleteKeyCode="Delete"`
**Change to:** `deleteKeyCode={["Backspace", "Delete"]}`

ReactFlow's `deleteKeyCode` prop accepts an array. This handles both Mac (Backspace) and Windows (Delete) keyboards.

**Why:** Mac keyboards do not have a "Delete" key; they have "Backspace". The current code makes node deletion via keyboard impossible on Mac.

---

#### Fix 7: "New Orchestration" Action

**File:** `/Users/yashbaldha/Desktop/Boldteq App/claude-hub/client/src/pages/Orchestration.tsx`

**Add a new function** (near `clearCanvas` at line 315):
```ts
const newOrchestration = () => {
  setNodes([])
  setEdges([])
  setOrchId(null)
  setOrchName('My Orchestration')
  setRunLog([])
  setSelectedNodeId(null)
  setTask('')
  setShowRunPanel(false)
  setViewingOutputNodeId(null)
}
```

**Add a "New" button to the toolbar** (line 494, before the Clear button):
```tsx
<button
  onClick={newOrchestration}
  className="flex items-center gap-2 px-3 py-1.5 text-sm font-medium rounded-lg border border-border hover:bg-surface-2 transition-colors"
  title="New orchestration"
>
  <Plus className="w-3.5 h-3.5" />
  New
</button>
```

**Why:** After loading a saved orchestration or completing a run, users have no clean way to start fresh. The "Clear" button requires a confirmation modal and doesn't reset the name/task/run state.

---

#### Fix 8: Post-Run State Management (Re-run / Reset)

**File:** `/Users/yashbaldha/Desktop/Boldteq App/claude-hub/client/src/pages/Orchestration.tsx`
**Location:** Lines 671-687 (the button area in the run panel)

**Change:** After the run completes (`!running && runLog.length > 0`), show "Re-run" and "Reset" buttons:

```tsx
<div className="flex gap-2 mt-3">
  {!running && runLog.length > 0 ? (
    <>
      <button
        onClick={handleRun}
        disabled={!task.trim() || nodes.length === 0}
        className="flex-1 flex items-center justify-center gap-2 px-4 py-2.5 bg-accent text-white rounded-xl text-sm font-medium hover:bg-accent-hover disabled:opacity-40 disabled:cursor-not-allowed transition-colors"
      >
        <Play className="w-4 h-4" /> Re-run
      </button>
      <button
        onClick={() => {
          setRunLog([]);
          setNodes(nds => nds.map(n => ({ ...n, data: { ...n.data, status: 'idle', output: undefined } })));
        }}
        className="px-3 py-2.5 border border-border rounded-xl text-sm font-medium hover:bg-surface-2 transition-colors"
      >
        Reset
      </button>
    </>
  ) : (
    <>
      <button
        onClick={handleRun}
        disabled={!task.trim() || running || nodes.length === 0}
        className="flex-1 flex items-center justify-center gap-2 px-4 py-2.5 bg-accent text-white rounded-xl text-sm font-medium hover:bg-accent-hover disabled:opacity-40 disabled:cursor-not-allowed transition-colors"
      >
        {running ? <><Loader className="w-4 h-4 animate-spin" /> Running…</> : <><Play className="w-4 h-4" /> Start Run</>}
      </button>
      {running && (
        <button
          onClick={cancelRun}
          className="px-3 py-2.5 border border-red/40 text-red rounded-xl text-sm font-medium hover:bg-red-muted transition-colors"
        >
          Cancel
        </button>
      )}
    </>
  )}
